# Clover — the mat. The lowest thing in the set, and the only trefoil.
#
# This is the plains-and-meadow ground cover, so it has the `sea_grass` problem
# doubled: it stands on green, it will be placed by the hundred, and `BlockModels`
# normalises by the Y extent. Anything with a vertical axis becomes a lawn you
# cannot see over. So clover is authored as a **mat** — five leaves splayed out
# and barely off the floor, `assert width / height > 1.3` — and the only thing
# with any height at all is the pair of flower heads, which exist precisely so
# the patch is not a flat green disc from the player's eye line.
#
# **The trefoil is the entire read.** Nothing else about clover is distinctive:
# the colour is grass green, the size is nothing, the stems are wire. What says
# clover is three rounded lobes meeting at a point, and if those three lobes
# merge into one blob at 32px the block is a smear. Hence the whole geometry
# budget goes into keeping them separate — each leaf is three *individual*
# flattened lofts fanned 115° apart around its stem tip, not one wide loft with
# a scalloped edge, and each lobe tapers back to 0.035 at its own base so there
# is real background visible in the notches between them. The
# `assert dist(...)` battery below measures those gaps as numbers rather than
# trusting a look at the sheet.
#
# **The lobes lie nearly flat and the stems do the leaning.** A trefoil is only a
# trefoil from above, and the sheet's three-quarter view is the closest thing the
# game camera has to that. So each petiole leaves the crown at a steep outward
# tilt (55–62°) — that is what makes the patch wide — and then the lobes it
# carries sit at a shallow `pitch` of a few degrees, near-horizontal in world
# space. The first cut hung the lobes off the stem's own direction and they
# stood on edge like little sails; from above the patch was five green lines.
#
# `frame=up` on every lobe, for the reason `sea_grass.wam` gives: a flattened
# loft needs its depth axis pinned to world up or a nearly-horizontal part sits
# right on the compiler's 45° auto-frame switch and half the lobes come out
# standing on their edges. That was the actual bug, not a stylistic choice.
#
# **Four sides and three rings per lobe.** A clover leaflet wants a round outline
# and six sides would give one, but fifteen lobes at six sides came out at 768
# triangles for a block that will be instanced across an entire biome. Four sides
# with a `dome` end cap and one wide middle ring reads round enough at the size
# this is ever seen, and it lands at 648.
#
# **The sheet said "oars".** The first compile had lobes 0.30 long and 0.16 wide
# and the mat read as a ring of little green paddles — the ratio, not the layout,
# was the whole problem. They are now 0.22 by 0.24, i.e. slightly broader than
# they are long, which is what a clover leaflet actually is, and the
# `assert depth(lf1a) > width(lf1a) * 0.62` is there so nobody stretches them
# back out. `lf1a` is the lobe to measure because its petiole sits at yaw 0, so
# its bounding box's `width` is honestly its length.
#
# **The flower heads were banded and it was a mistake.** Pink at 0.24, white at
# 0.60, pink at 0.88 was meant to break the head into floret rows; on the sheet
# it read as a horizontal stripe round a beach ball from all four views, because
# a band on a low-poly sphere is a band, not a texture. Reverted to white with a
# single pink crown at the top, which is both what the plant looks like and the
# only version where the head still reads as one round object.
#
# The flower heads are white going pink at the crown, on stems twice the leaves'
# length: white is the one value that is guaranteed to separate from grass in
# every light, and putting them well above the mat means the patch still reads
# from a standing player who cannot see the trefoils at all.

model clover
  height 0.24
  style chunky

palette
  Leaf  #5f9440
  Deep  #47752f
  Stem  #6ea24a
  Bloom #f4f0e6
  Pink  #edb9c8

skeleton
  root soil at 0.0
  # Five petioles on five headings, 72° apart. On an `up` bone a negative `tilt`
  # leans toward +X and `yaw` swings that lean around the circle (yaw=90 sends
  # +X to -Z), so the pair (tilt, yaw) is "how far out" and "which way" — which
  # is exactly the two decisions a splayed stem is. Each is rooted a little way
  # out along its own heading, so no petiole straddles X=0 lopsidedly.
  bone pet1 parent=soil side=0.045                dir=up tilt=-60 yaw=0   len=0.30
  bone pet2 parent=soil side=0.030 fwd=-0.043     dir=up tilt=-56 yaw=72  len=0.27
  bone pet3 parent=soil side=-0.036 fwd=-0.026    dir=up tilt=-62 yaw=144 len=0.29
  bone pet4 parent=soil side=-0.036 fwd=0.026     dir=up tilt=-58 yaw=216 len=0.26
  bone pet5 parent=soil side=0.014 fwd=0.043      dir=up tilt=-61 yaw=288 len=0.28
  # Two flower stalks. Near-vertical and long — these are the only part of the
  # model with a Y axis worth speaking of, and they set the model's height.
  bone fst1 parent=soil side=0.020 fwd=0.018 dir=up tilt=-12 pitch=9  len=0.44
  bone fst2 parent=soil side=-0.026 fwd=-0.020 dir=up tilt=14 pitch=-7 len=0.36

parts
  # Petioles. Wire — three rings, four sides. The flare at the foot is all that
  # keeps them from looking pushed into the ground rather than grown out of it.
  loft st1 bones=pet1..pet1 material=Stem sides=4
    ring 0.00 w=0.040 d=0.038
    ring 0.14 w=0.022 d=0.021
    ring 1.00 w=0.018 d=0.017
    cap start=flat end=flat
  loft st2 bones=pet2..pet2 material=Stem sides=4
    ring 0.00 w=0.038 d=0.036
    ring 0.14 w=0.021 d=0.020
    ring 1.00 w=0.017 d=0.016
    cap start=flat end=flat
  loft st3 bones=pet3..pet3 material=Stem sides=4
    ring 0.00 w=0.040 d=0.038
    ring 0.14 w=0.022 d=0.021
    ring 1.00 w=0.018 d=0.017
    cap start=flat end=flat
  loft st4 bones=pet4..pet4 material=Stem sides=4
    ring 0.00 w=0.038 d=0.036
    ring 0.14 w=0.021 d=0.020
    ring 1.00 w=0.017 d=0.016
    cap start=flat end=flat
  loft st5 bones=pet5..pet5 material=Stem sides=4
    ring 0.00 w=0.038 d=0.036
    ring 0.14 w=0.021 d=0.020
    ring 1.00 w=0.017 d=0.016
    cap start=flat end=flat

  # Leaf 1 — the trefoil, three lobes off one petiole tip.
  #
  # Headings: a `fwd` bone yawed by ψ points at (sin ψ, 0, cos ψ), and a petiole
  # yawed by φ runs out along (cos φ, 0, −sin φ), so the lobe that continues
  # straight out is ψ = 90 + φ and the two flankers are that ±115°. 115 rather
  # than the botanical 120 because at 120 the two back lobes sit exactly astride
  # the petiole and hide it; four degrees of asymmetry is invisible and buys a
  # clean stem.
  #
  # The narrow ring 0 is the notch: three lobes 0.035 wide where they meet leave
  # actual daylight between them, and daylight is what makes three shapes read as
  # three shapes.
  loft lf1a bone=pet1 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=90  pitch=5   len=0.22 on=st1 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.035 d=0.020
    ring 0.52 w=0.240 d=0.026
    ring 1.00 w=0.149 d=0.018
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf1b bone=pet1 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=-25 pitch=4   len=0.19 on=st1 material=Deep sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.035 d=0.020
    ring 0.52 w=0.224 d=0.024
    ring 1.00 w=0.139 d=0.017
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf1c bone=pet1 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=205 pitch=6   len=0.19 on=st1 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.035 d=0.020
    ring 0.52 w=0.224 d=0.024
    ring 1.00 w=0.139 d=0.017
    cap start=flat end=dome

  # Leaf 2 — petiole yaw 72, so the lobes sit at 162 and 162±115.
  loft lf2a bone=pet2 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=162 pitch=4   len=0.20 on=st2 material=Deep sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.034 d=0.019
    ring 0.52 w=0.232 d=0.025
    ring 1.00 w=0.144 d=0.017
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf2b bone=pet2 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=47  pitch=6   len=0.18 on=st2 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.034 d=0.019
    ring 0.52 w=0.217 d=0.023
    ring 1.00 w=0.135 d=0.016
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf2c bone=pet2 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=277 pitch=3   len=0.18 on=st2 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.034 d=0.019
    ring 0.52 w=0.217 d=0.023
    ring 1.00 w=0.135 d=0.016
    cap start=flat end=dome

  # Leaf 3 — petiole yaw 144.
  loft lf3a bone=pet3 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=234 pitch=5   len=0.21 on=st3 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.035 d=0.020
    ring 0.52 w=0.236 d=0.026
    ring 1.00 w=0.146 d=0.018
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf3b bone=pet3 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=119 pitch=4   len=0.18 on=st3 material=Deep sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.034 d=0.019
    ring 0.52 w=0.221 d=0.024
    ring 1.00 w=0.138 d=0.017
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf3c bone=pet3 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=349 pitch=6   len=0.18 on=st3 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.034 d=0.019
    ring 0.52 w=0.221 d=0.024
    ring 1.00 w=0.138 d=0.017
    cap start=flat end=dome

  # Leaf 4 — petiole yaw 216.
  loft lf4a bone=pet4 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=306 pitch=4   len=0.19 on=st4 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.034 d=0.019
    ring 0.52 w=0.227 d=0.025
    ring 1.00 w=0.141 d=0.017
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf4b bone=pet4 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=191 pitch=6   len=0.17 on=st4 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.033 d=0.018
    ring 0.52 w=0.213 d=0.023
    ring 1.00 w=0.133 d=0.016
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf4c bone=pet4 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=61  pitch=3   len=0.17 on=st4 material=Deep sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.033 d=0.018
    ring 0.52 w=0.213 d=0.023
    ring 1.00 w=0.133 d=0.016
    cap start=flat end=dome

  # Leaf 5 — petiole yaw 288.
  loft lf5a bone=pet5 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=18  pitch=5   len=0.20 on=st5 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.034 d=0.019
    ring 0.52 w=0.232 d=0.025
    ring 1.00 w=0.144 d=0.017
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf5b bone=pet5 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=263 pitch=4   len=0.18 on=st5 material=Leaf sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.034 d=0.019
    ring 0.52 w=0.217 d=0.023
    ring 1.00 w=0.135 d=0.016
    cap start=flat end=dome
  loft lf5c bone=pet5 at=1.0 dir=fwd yaw=133 pitch=6   len=0.18 on=st5 material=Deep sides=4 frame=up
    ring 0.00 w=0.034 d=0.019
    ring 0.52 w=0.217 d=0.023
    ring 1.00 w=0.135 d=0.016
    cap start=flat end=dome

  # The two flower stalks and their heads. Thinner than the petioles — a clover
  # flower rides a wire — and the heads are `squarish` with alternating `roll=`
  # for the flower-clump reason: a smooth sphere at this size is a berry, and
  # what a clover head actually is, is a ball of florets.
  loft fs1 bones=fst1..fst1 material=Stem sides=4
    ring 0.00 w=0.032 d=0.030
    ring 0.14 w=0.018 d=0.017
    ring 1.00 w=0.015 d=0.014
    cap start=flat end=flat
  loft fs2 bones=fst2..fst2 material=Stem sides=4
    ring 0.00 w=0.030 d=0.028
    ring 0.14 w=0.017 d=0.016
    ring 1.00 w=0.014 d=0.013
    cap start=flat end=flat
  loft head1 bone=fst1 at=1.0 dir=up pitch=-5 len=0.17 on=fs1 material=Bloom sides=6 shape=squarish
    ring 0.00 w=0.055 d=0.052 material=Bloom
    ring 0.24 w=0.145 d=0.138 roll=18
    ring 0.60 w=0.155 d=0.148 roll=-20
    ring 0.88 w=0.110 d=0.105 roll=16 material=Pink
    ring 1.00 w=0.045 d=0.043
    cap start=flat end=flat
  loft head2 bone=fst2 at=1.0 dir=up pitch=7 len=0.15 on=fs2 material=Bloom sides=6 shape=squarish
    ring 0.00 w=0.050 d=0.048 material=Bloom
    ring 0.24 w=0.132 d=0.126 roll=-19
    ring 0.60 w=0.142 d=0.135 roll=21
    ring 0.88 w=0.100 d=0.095 roll=-15 material=Pink
    ring 1.00 w=0.040 d=0.038
    cap start=flat end=flat

checks
  # A mat, not a plant. This is the check the whole model exists to satisfy, and
  # the one that fails the moment somebody lengthens a flower stalk to make the
  # block more visible. It is much wider than tall — 1.3 is the floor, not a
  # target.
  assert width / height > 1.3
  assert depth / height > 1.3
  # Rooted. Every petiole and both flower stalks start in the soil.
  assert bottom(st1) < 0.02
  assert bottom(st3) < 0.02
  assert bottom(fs1) < 0.02
  assert bottom(fs2) < 0.02
  # The trefoil, stated as arithmetic. Three lobes on one stem tip, each of them
  # substantially longer than it is thick, all three fanned far enough apart that
  # their far ends are further from each other than one lobe is wide. If the
  # lobes are ever fattened into a single blob this is what catches it.
  assert dist(lf1a, lf1b) > width(lf1a) * 0.75
  assert dist(lf1b, lf1c) > width(lf1b) * 0.75
  assert dist(lf3a, lf3b) > width(lf3a) * 0.75
  assert dist(lf5b, lf5c) > width(lf5b) * 0.75
  # Lobes lie flat: each one is far thinner top-to-bottom than it is across, so
  # the trefoil is visible from above rather than edge-on. This is the check that
  # would have caught the sails.
  assert height(lf1a) < width(lf1a) / 2.5
  assert height(lf3a) < width(lf3a) / 2.5
  assert height(lf4a) < width(lf4a) / 2.5
  # The leaf mat stays below the flowers — the whole point of putting the heads
  # on long stalks is that they clear the leaves and can be seen standing up.
  assert top(head1) > top(lf1a) * 1.5
  assert bottom(head1) > top(lf1a)
  assert top(head1) > top(head2)
  # ...and the heads are the tallest thing in the model, so the Y extent (which
  # `BlockModels` normalises by) is set by them and not by an over-tilted leaf.
  assert top(head1) > top(lf3a)
  assert top(head1) > top(lf5a)
  # White reads against grass; the head has to be big enough to see. Stated as a
  # flat number and not as a multiple of `width(fs1)`, which was the first cut
  # and immediately failed: the flower stalk is tilted, so its *bounding box*
  # width is a tenth of the model rather than the 0.015 the tube actually is.
  # There is no expression for "how thick the part is", so the bound goes on the
  # thing that matters directly — the head is at least a sixth of the model
  # across, which at icon size is a visible white dot.
  assert width(head1) > 0.13
  assert width(head1) > width(head2)
  # Round lobes, not oars. `lf1a` points along +X by construction (its petiole is
  # at yaw 0), so `width` is its length and `depth` is how broad it is — the
  # first sheet had those at 0.31 by 0.16 and the mat read as a patch of little
  # green paddles. A clover leaflet is nearly as broad as it is long.
  assert depth(lf1a) > width(lf1a) * 0.62
  assert depth(lf1a) < width(lf1a) * 1.30
  assert parts == 24
  assert tris < 700
  measure spread width / height
  measure lobegap dist(lf1a, lf1b)
